title: SleepyRunner Generalized

files:
  - .assets/img-104-03-01.png
  - em.mark:em.mark/ActivityI.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark.busywait/BusyWaitA.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark/RunnerP.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark.busywait/BusyWaitC.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark.coremark/CoreMarkA.em.ts

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cx]em.mark.*% Architecture}

      Our generalization of {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} organizes bench&shy;mark elements across a family of `em.mark.*` buckets.

      The {[cu]RunnerP} program {[dc,1]} anchors this architecture by calling {[cu]ActivityI} delegates {[dc,2]} at run-time.

      <div class="em-info">

      Each conformant **Delegate** implements the actual work&shy;load defined by a particular benchmark.

      </div>

      A corresponding set of EMS **Composites** {[dc,3]} orchestrate _build-time_ configuration and binding of {[dc,1]} and {[dc,2]}.

      This tour first examines these elements, then sets the stage for defining additional benchmark activities.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]ActivityI% &ndash; Quick Look}

      The {[cu]ActivityI} **Interface** (found in the `em.mark` **Bucket**) defines the run-time behavior of benchmark workloads.

      Besides implementing the specified functions, a conform&shy;ing **Delegate** often exports additional config parameters.

      <div class="em-info">

      Depending upon the {[cu]ActivityI} implementation, the {[cf]setup} and {[cf]teardown} functions may remain empty.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BusyWaitA% &ndash; Details}

  - cmds:
      - open 4
    focus: [4, 1]
    text: |
